./working_notes/bibliography.md:43:- `iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md` and critique: refined the reward-measure domination no-gap proof. The compactness argument works for completed \(L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\) public-prefix kernels, but original Borel private-randomized policies still need policy-to-prefix-kernel reduction and Borel/completed implementation lemmas.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:44:- `iterations/iter_009/idea_02/explorer_01.md` and critique: strengthened the non-Borel finite-subclass/full-class obstruction to a linear-in-\(T\) deterministic market example on \(G=\{1/3,2/3\}\), with finite-subclass value \(0\) and full Borel-policy value \((T-1)/6\); confirmed it remains a measurability pathology rather than a natural Borel statistical gap.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:47:- Exact finite-experts delayed-feedback theorem if choosing citation over inline proof.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:51:- Finite statistical decision/minimax theorem for least-favorable priors over finite model classes with standard Borel observations and randomized kernels, if using an external citation instead of proving the risk-vector minimax argument inline.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:53:- Measure-theoretic references may be useful for the iteration 8 no-gap theorem: Banach-Alaoglu/weak-* compactness for \(L^\infty=(L^1)^*\), measurable-version results for kernels on standard Borel spaces, and the countable-total-variation-dense family implies common domination principle.
