./working_notes/promising_directions.md:84:Critical checks: Separate the relaxed-kernel theorem from the original Borel private-randomized policy theorem. For the latter, prove policy-to-prefix-kernel reduction by induction over public prices, private randomness, memory, and delayed feedback; prove a Borel/completed implementation lemma for weak-* limit kernels; use the same policy class in finite-subclass and full-class values; identify the density support formula with the conditional-probability \(\Psi_{G,F}\). Keep continuum quotes behind the \(T/K\) grid comparison. Record compactness/measurability gap examples as obstructions, not natural lower bounds.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:89:Critical checks: State TV separability for finite measures, not weak/Feller continuity. Dominated approximation needs explicit constants and uniformity over all policies. Identifiable classes need standard Borel parameter spaces, measurable decoding, and measurable finite-grid argmax.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:100:Critical checks: Define the decision-time sigma-field, independence of private randomness, regular conditional probabilities, and public exogenous observations. For \([0,1]\), use \(\varepsilon\)-selectors or a grid approximation.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:115:Critical checks: Avoid direct \(\ell^\infty\) duality unless finitely additive priors are intended. Weak/Feller continuity alone is not enough for Borel-policy no-gap; verify that any compactness assumption is on the attainable reward set or implies product closedness. Keep identifiable nondominated examples separate from compactness proofs. Iteration 9 found only a non-Borel linear gap; the next counterexample search should target Borel, nondominated, non-identifiable classes, since Borel identifiable labels and countable identifier sets are easy.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:117:## 14. Keep the linear non-Borel gap in the obstruction suite
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:118:Goal: Use the \(T=D+1\), \(G=\{1/3,2/3\}\) non-Borel label construction as the standard stress test for arbitrary infinite-class finite-subclass claims.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:119:Why promising: It shows the finite-subclass modulus can be off by \(\Theta(T)\) for Borel policies, so no-gap/product-closed hypotheses are not merely cosmetic.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:120:Critical checks: Always label it a measurability pathology. It vanishes for arbitrary nonmeasurable policies, Borel \(A\), or countable identifiers, and it does not contradict reward-measure domination because domination fails.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:5:Failure: False under law-aware/process-wise quantifiers. For iid uniform prices, \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but a learner knowing the law can play the best fixed quote in expectation and get zero regret. Iteration 2 strengthened this: for every finite-horizon known exogenous law, a horizon-aware conditional grid oracle gets regret at most \(T/K\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:19:## Nontrivial fixed-law necessity under known finite-horizon exogenous laws
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:20:Attempt: Find any non-vacuous tail, truncation, or delay condition necessary for sublinear regret when the learner knows the finite-horizon exogenous law.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:22:Lesson: Any meaningful necessity theorem must change quantifiers: unknown/minimax class, action-dependent prices, computational constraints, or anytime horizon-free policies.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:34:Lesson: Upper theorem must assume exogenous/oblivious prices, or explicitly define an action-dependent causal model and prove a different reduction.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:50:Attempt: Declare the finite-horizon exogenous minimax problem solved by the least-favorable Bayes predictability gap
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:60:Failure: Iteration 4 critics rejected this as an asserted minimax theorem, not a proof. The displayed formula also mixed a claimed finite-grid result with continuum comparator/envelope quantities, ignored strict-threshold nonattainment, and did not specify compactness, regular conditional probabilities, randomized policies, or a negative-regret convention.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:65:Attempt: Stop the loop by asserting the corrected finite-grid minimax identity from an informal weak-* compactness/Sion argument over public-prefix kernels, plus a one-line continuum rounding claim.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:66:Failure: The solution gate rejected this as not referee-ready. The sketch did not precisely define the policy-space topology, prove closedness/compactness of the kernel constraints, handle measurable representatives, or prove the two-sided continuum comparison. It also overstated the result as a necessary-and-sufficient tail characterization, though it is a minimax-value characterization.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:76:Failure: The one-line argument does not by itself compare comparator values, learner policy classes, minimax values, and the finite-grid Bayes envelope. The comparison is valid only after proving both minimax inequalities
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:98:Failure: The equivalence is valid only under finite \(\Theta_T\), public exogenous prices, randomized policies, and the grid approximation. The quantity \(\sup_\pi\Psi_{G_T}\) is essentially a minimax value/Bayes predictability gap, not a scalar tail profile. It does not cover infinite classes without compactness or approximation, and it does not give matching power-tail rates.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:104:Failure: The finite-subclass modulus equals the minimax value over the product-closure of the attainable reward set, not necessarily over the attainable set itself. Abstractly, \(C=\{c\in[0,1]^{\mathbb N}:c\text{ has finite support}\}\) with \(V\equiv1\) has every finite-subclass value \(0\) but full value \(1\). A pathological market-flavored non-Borel label example gives the same warning under Borel-policy restrictions.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:105:Lesson: Infinite-class extensions need a no-gap condition, such as product closedness of \(C\). Common prefix domination is a promising sufficient condition, but the policy-kernel and measurability setup must be written explicitly.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:108:## Ignoring randomized policies in minimax/Bayes identities
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:110:Failure: A two-world one-decision test separates deterministic and randomized minimax values. With \(T=2\), \(P_1=0\), grid \(G=\{0.5,0.9\}\), and worlds \(P_2=0.6\) or \(P_2=1\), deterministic minimax regret is \(0.4\), while randomized play balances the two regrets at \(2/9\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:111:Lesson: Any minimax/Bayes equality must explicitly allow randomized policies or mixed decision kernels.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:121:Attempt: Use the known-law conditional oracle to claim all known fixed infinite exogenous processes are trivial for a single policy that does not know the evaluation horizon.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:140:Failure: Both solution critics and the gate rejected this as a full solution. The finite-class value theorem is accepted, but arbitrary infinite classes need an explicit no-gap/product-closed condition and a precise policy space. The dominated-prefix route was still conditional, not written referee-ready. The theorem is a minimax/Bayes value characterization, not a scalar tail characterization, and no matching lower bound for the power-tail upper rate is known.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:146:Failure: Iteration 8 found no proof that weak/Feller continuity alone makes the Borel-policy attainable reward set product closed. Uncountable Dirac classes can be weakly compact or Feller-continuous while failing total-variation separability and common domination. Deterministic identifiable examples may still be easy, but for a different reason: the parameter is publicly decoded, not because the reward set is compact in the needed topology.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:151:Attempt: Use common domination of every public prefix law \(P^\theta_{1:t}\) as the main checkable hypothesis for infinite-class no-gap.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:157:Lesson: State prefix-law domination as a simple sufficient condition, but promote payoff-weighted reward-measure domination as the sharper no-gap hypothesis. Still prove policy-to-prefix-kernel reduction carefully so ignoring zero-current-payoff prefixes is legitimate.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:160:## Treating the \(L^\infty\) kernel compactness theorem as an original Borel-policy theorem
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:161:Attempt: Close the dominated reward-measure no-gap theorem by proving weak-* compactness of \(L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\) prefix kernels and then directly identifying this with the original class of private-randomized Borel public-price policies.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:162:Failure: Iteration 9's critic agreed that the compactness/no-gap proof works for the completed \(L^\infty\) prefix-kernel policy class, but not that this class has been proved identical to the intended original policy class. Two directions remain: every private-randomized policy must induce law-independent public-prefix marginal kernels, and every weak-* limit kernel must have an admissible Borel/completed implementation.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:163:Lesson: State the no-gap theorem first for the relaxed completed kernel model. Add a separate policy-class alignment lemma with exact assumptions on public prices, private randomness, delayed feedback, Borel versions, and null-set conventions before claiming it for original Borel policies.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:167:Attempt: Find a natural public-exogenous infinite class where all finite subclasses have small grid minimax value but the full class has positive or linear value, avoiding non-Borel labels.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:168:Failure: Iteration 9 found only a strengthened non-Borel label obstruction. In identifiable deterministic templates, if the optimal-action label is Borel, a Borel policy can decode \(x=P_1\) and play the right quote; if identifiers are countable, every label subset is Borel. Dominated or TV-separable payoff-weighted reward-measure classes are already covered by the no-gap theorem.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:169:Lesson: The linear non-Borel construction is useful as a measurability/product-closure warning, but not evidence of a natural Borel statistical gap. The next search should target Borel, nondominated, non-identifiable classes or accept that product-closure failures found so far are pathological.
./iterations/iter_009/promising_ideas.json:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The proof may only establish compactness for L-infinity equivalence classes rather than admissible Borel policies. Null sets and measurable representatives may not align across theta. The reduction may also hide assumptions about private randomness and delayed feedback.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measurability audit","critic stress test"],"summary":"Write a referee-ready proof of the fixed finite T, finite grid G reward-measure domination theorem. Show any private-randomized public-price policy has the same expected rewards as prefix kernels q_t(P_1:t), derive L_theta(q) from payoff-weighted measures, prove weak-star compactness of the feasible kernel set, and conclude product compactness/no-gap.","title":"Close The No-Gap Proof","why_promising":"This is the main conditional gap in the current solver attempt. If it works, the finite-class Bayes value theorem extends cleanly to dominated infinite classes."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Actual market reward sets may be too structured to realize the abstract finite-support pathology. Countable or Borel-identifiable versions may be easy for universal policies, collapsing the obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","model realization","measurability audit"],"summary":"Search for a natural public-exogenous infinite class where every finite subclass has small grid minimax value but the full class has positive or linear minimax value. Avoid relying on non-Borel labels if possible; otherwise prove that any standard-Borel attempt must fail under identifiable or dominated assumptions.","title":"Natural Infinite-Class Gap","why_promising":"It would clarify whether product closedness is a real mathematical assumption or mostly a technical artifact of the current proof."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Uniform approximation over all policies may be essentially total-variation strength and therefore too restrictive. Comparator suprema may amplify small measure errors by order T.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","constant tracking","counterexample search"],"summary":"Prove an approximate no-gap theorem: if each reward measure and grid comparator value is uniformly approximated by a dominated surrogate game within epsilon_T, bound the true full-class grid value by the finite-subclass Bayes modulus plus an explicit constant times epsilon_T. Identify the weakest useful metric on the payoff-weighted measures.","title":"Approximate Domination","why_promising":"This could turn the exact domination theorem into a robust tool for realistic infinite classes and finite approximations."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Measurable decoder and argmax assumptions may be nontrivial. If decode time grows with T, early regret may dominate. Partial or noisy decoding likely falls back to the Bayes-gap framework.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measurable selection","examples"],"summary":"Formalize an orthogonal theorem for nondominated classes where a public prefix decodes theta after m steps. Under standard Borel and measurable oracle assumptions, bound regret by the pre-decode loss plus O(T/K), or compute the exact pre-decode Bayes gap.","title":"Identifiable Singular Classes","why_promising":"Common domination excludes many deterministic Dirac-like classes that are intuitively easy once the parameter is publicly revealed."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best fixed quote in expectation may collapse to the Bayes action. Future spikes can trade old quotes and couple blocks. Hidden bits may reveal too much, and the construction may violate the target tail envelope.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound construction","Bayes-gap computation","counterexample search"],"summary":"Use the finite-grid Psi_G formula to design horizon-indexed finite classes satisfying a tail envelope B(h), especially B(h)=h^{-alpha}, with accumulated Bayes gap near the truncation upper rate. Explore Assouad or Fano-style hidden bits across scales, limited-leakage terminal observations, and quote intervals that prevent one reveal from solving all blocks.","title":"Multi-Scale Tail Lower Bounds","why_promising":"Matching power-tail lower rates are the largest open quantitative gap; the known one-block qD atom is too weak."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting quantity may be tautological, essentially restating the minimax value. Infinite-class product-closure issues would still remain, and the modulus may be hard to estimate.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","upper-lower comparison","toy examples"],"summary":"Define and test a tail-like predictability modulus based on full versus H-truncated least-favorable Bayes value, such as the gap between full and truncated Psi_G over priors or between full and truncated minimax values. Compare it to delta_T(H), Delta_T(H), and raw beta(H) on the known examples.","title":"Bayes-Truncation Modulus","why_promising":"It may bridge the accepted truncation upper theorem with the minimax/Bayes characterization while avoiding false raw delay tails."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"A randomized mixture or doubling-style schedule may avoid the proposed lower bound. Later blocks may contaminate horizon calculations. This is separate from the horizon-known model if that is the final scope.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search","model taxonomy"],"summary":"Resolve the horizon-free known-law regime. Formalize the super-growing two-deadline deterministic sequence stress test and prove that any single policy knowing the infinite sequence but not T has linear regret on a subsequence, or find an anytime policy that defeats the construction.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Boundary","why_promising":"The known-law oracle is horizon-aware; this determines whether fixed-process learnability stays trivial without horizon knowledge."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Several causal regret notions are plausible, so the work may become model design rather than solving the exogenous problem. The result may not inform the main theorem if action-dependent prices are declared out of scope.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search","causal semantics"],"summary":"Write a minimal action-dependent price protocol and either construct a counterexample to the delayed full-information truncation reduction or prove an analogue under strong nonanticipativity. Explicitly define whether comparator rewards use learner-induced or comparator-induced price paths.","title":"Action-Dependent Audit","why_promising":"All accepted upper and value-characterization results require public exogenous prices; this closes a major quantifier ambiguity in the original problem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_008/solution_gate.md:5:The finite-class core is credible as partial progress: for finite \(\Theta_T\), finite grids, public exogenous prices, randomized measurable policies, and positive-part upper regret, the Bayes/minimax value characterization plus \(T/K\) discretization appears accepted in the working notes. But the solver packages this as a broader “necessary-and-sufficient characterization” while leaving key assumptions conditional.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_gate.md:9:- Infinite classes: the finitely supported prior formula is valid only under a precise product-closed/no-gap condition on the attainable reward set. The solver does not prove this condition or fully specify the policy space.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_gate.md:13:- Scope: action-dependent prices, anytime policies, arbitrary nondominated infinite classes, and high-probability/pathwise regret remain outside the proof.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_gate.md:15:Remaining assumptions for the accepted partial theorem include: public exogenous action-independent prices; known horizon \(T\); randomized measurable policies with independent private randomness; finite \(\Theta_T\) or a proved product-closed no-gap condition; grid-restricted \(\Psi_{T,G}\); signed upper-regret with positive-part learnability; regular conditional probabilities; and continuum handled only through discretization, not an exact continuum Bayes formula.
./iterations/iter_005/promising_ideas.json:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The weak-* policy-space argument may hide a measurability or continuity gap, especially in reducing arbitrary randomized histories to kernels depending only on public price prefixes. Sion may also be unnecessary but misapplied if compactness is not stated in the right topology.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","functional analysis audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Prove or refute the finite-Theta, finite-grid minimax identity exactly as stated in the solver attempt. Focus on the reduction to public-prefix randomized kernels, construction of the compact convex policy space as products of L-infinity simplices dominated by prefix mixture measures, weak-* continuity of each L_theta, and the Sion/von Neumann minimax step.","title":"Audit finite-grid minimax identity","why_promising":"This is the solver attempt's central new claim. If it survives, it gives a precise necessary-and-sufficient finite-class characterization via least-favorable Bayes gap; if it fails, the failure point will sharply delimit what can be claimed."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"A classical theorem may require assumptions not present here, such as finite observation spaces, dominated experiments, compact action spaces with continuity, or nonnegative loss conventions. It may also prove only epsilon-minimax or value existence, not the exact displayed Psi_G formula.","suggested_lenses":["literature/theorem matching","proof strategy"],"summary":"Find the cleanest finite statistical decision theorem that implies the identity: finite parameter set, standard Borel observations/histories, finite action grid at each time, randomized decision kernels, bounded loss. Translate the delayed-market problem into that theorem and identify the Bayes risk term using the already accepted fixed-prior myopic lemma.","title":"Decision-theoretic shortcut","why_promising":"This could replace the solver's informal compactness proof with a standard minimax/least-favorable-prior result, making the final write-up shorter and more defensible."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The bound may be T/K, 2T/K, or require separate one-sided inequalities depending on whether learner actions, comparator values, and Bayes envelopes are rounded consistently. Strict threshold nonattainment can break exact continuum statements if suprema are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","edge-case audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify the grid-to-continuum approximation claimed by the solver. Prove both directions comparing R_[0,1] and R_GK using downward rounding, then check whether sup_pi Psi_GK approximates any continuum Bayes-gap object and whether positive-part upper-regret changes the statement.","title":"Continuum approximation check","why_promising":"Even if the finite-grid identity is true, the problem's original quote space is [0,1]. This direction isolates the discretization and sign-convention details that previously caused false claims."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Finite-tree computations may only test simplified models and could miss pathologies of general standard Borel histories. Conversely, a counterexample may expose a definition mismatch rather than invalidate the intended theorem.","suggested_lenses":["computational verification","counterexample search"],"summary":"Build exact finite observation-tree instances and compute the minimax value by linear programming, then compute sup_pi Psi_G directly. Include the known stress tests: deterministic-vs-randomized two-world example, negative regret under adaptive known-law play, grid/continuum mismatch, and hidden-terminal atoms.","title":"Finite LP stress tests","why_promising":"Small exact games can quickly catch sign errors, missing randomization, bad comparator conventions, or incorrect Bayes-envelope formulas before explorers invest in an abstract proof."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Previous iid block-splicing failed because the best fixed comparator is based on expected total reward, not realized per-block optima. Multi-scale constructions may again collapse to a Bayes-average quote unless quote levels, weights, and hidden types are arranged very carefully.","suggested_lenses":["lower-bound construction","Bayes-gap computation","counterexample search"],"summary":"Use the finite-grid identity as a design tool for multi-scale lower bounds under a tail envelope B(h). Construct finite classes with hidden delayed value at several delay scales, compute Psi_G for candidate priors, and test whether the lower bound can approach the truncation upper rate T^((alpha+1)/(2alpha+1)) for B(h)=h^-alpha.","title":"Multi-scale Bayes-gap lower rates","why_promising":"The one-block qD atom is known to be too weak. The exact Bayes-gap value may reveal how to accumulate indistinguishable delayed value across scales without the fixed-comparator collapse."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Approximate indistinguishability bounds can accidentally upper-bound the wrong posterior envelope when reward curves depend on histories. KL/TV control may be too weak to preserve separated fixed-comparator values over enough rounds.","suggested_lenses":["Le Cam/Fano lower bound","information theory","proof strategy"],"summary":"Develop limited-leakage versions of the hidden-terminal lower bound where worlds are not identical-prefix but remain hard to distinguish for many decisions. Formalize a per-round Bayes reward upper bound using TV or KL distance between decision-history laws, then search for constructions with repeated small information leaks and valuable delayed rewards.","title":"Limited-leakage lower bounds","why_promising":"This is a plausible route to matching rates: instead of a single terminal reveal that teaches the world, keep posterior uncertainty alive while regret accumulates."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The obstruction may disappear under a doubling-style horizon-free policy or when regret is measured only at all sufficiently large horizons. The deterministic block parameters must be chosen so earlier blocks do not dominate or let the learner infer all future deadlines too easily.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","model taxonomy","proof strategy"],"summary":"Formalize the candidate anytime known-law obstruction using super-growing deterministic blocks with two possible deadline-relevant prices. Prove or refute that a single horizon-free policy for a known infinite exogenous sequence must incur linear regret along some horizon subsequence, despite every fixed finite horizon being oracle-learnable.","title":"Anytime known-law boundary","why_promising":"The solver's characterization is horizon-aware. This direction clarifies whether the final theorem needs a separate regime for horizon-free learnability, which the original problem's quantifiers may require."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"If the intended model is explicitly exogenous, this direction is only a scope note. If action dependence is allowed, the original regret definition itself may need causal counterfactual semantics before any theorem can be stated.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Audit whether the market protocol permits P_t to depend on the learner's past quotes. If yes, define comparator paths and counterfactual rewards precisely, then test whether the delayed full-information upper reduction and finite-grid Bayes identity fail through simple adaptive-price examples.","title":"Action-dependent market audit","why_promising":"All accepted upper and Bayes-value arguments rely on public exogenous prices. This direction prevents the final theorem from silently proving a result for the wrong protocol."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s proof strategy is worth pursuing and likely correct for a carefully defined relaxed policy class: fixed finite \(T\), finite grid \(G\), public exogenous prices, and admissible policies taken to be \(\lambda_t\)-measurable public-prefix kernels modulo dominated null sets.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:5:But it has not yet closed the main gap as stated. The hard point is still policy-class alignment: showing the compact \(L^\infty\) kernel object is exactly implementable by the original private-randomized Borel public-price policies, not just a relaxation.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:10:   The theorem claims no-gap for “randomized public-prefix grid policies,” but the compactness proof works over \(Q_t=L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\) equivalence classes. To prove the intended result, one needs both directions:
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:11:   - every admissible private-randomized policy induces such kernels with the same reward vector;
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:12:   - every weak-* limit kernel has an admissible Borel/completed-measure implementation.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:14:2. **[missing assumption] The prefix-kernel reduction needs an explicit policy model.**  
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:15:   Lemma 1 is valid only if private randomness is independent of the active law and price path, prices are exogenous, full public prices \(P_{1:t}\) are observed, and delayed trade feedback contains no extra information beyond public prices and past quotes.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:20:4. **[plausible but incomplete] Borel-version lemma is essential.**  
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:21:   For \(\lambda_t\)-measurable \(L^\infty\) kernels, one must prove existence of Borel representatives taking values in \(\Delta(G)\) outside a \(\lambda_t\)-null set, then set a default action on the null set. This should work on \([0,1]^t\) with sigma-finite Borel \(\lambda_t\), but it must be stated.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:26:6. **[missing assumption] Finite-subclass values must use the same policy class.**  
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:27:   The finite-intersection step compares \(\mathcal R_G(\Theta)\) to \(\sup_F\mathcal R_G(F)\). This is valid only if all \(\mathcal R_G(F)\) are computed over the same \(Q\)-implementable policy class, or if equality with the original Borel class has already been proved.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:46:- **Non-Borel label example:** still breaks arbitrary infinite-class claims without domination/product closedness.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:48:- **Action-dependent prices:** destroys the prefix-kernel/full-information reduction.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:49:- **Private randomness correlated with the law:** would make Lemma 1 false.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:50:- **Censored/private feedback instead of full public prices:** future decisions may depend on information not captured by \(P_{1:t}\).
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:58:The core compactness theorem survives under a clean relaxed-policy formulation. Payoff-weighted reward-measure domination should imply product compactness of the attainable reward set, hence no infinite-class finite-subclass gap on a finite grid. The finite-prior density formula is also a useful way to avoid conditional-probability version issues.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:62:1. Write the exact admissible policy definition.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:63:2. Prove policy-to-prefix-kernel reduction by induction.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:64:3. Prove the Borel/completed-measure implementation lemma for \(L^\infty\) kernels.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:65:4. Reprove finite-subclass no-gap using the same policy class throughout.
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:9:- The minimax step is not proved. Lines 77-94 assert weak-* compactness, weak-* continuity, and Sion applicability, but do not define the policy space/topology precisely or verify Sion’s hypotheses. This is the central theorem, not a technical detail.
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:11:- The reduction from arbitrary randomized policies to kernels \(q_t(\cdot\mid P_{1:t})\) is only asserted. It needs proof that past actions/private randomness carry no information about \(\theta\) beyond public prices, and that every such kernel family is implementable.
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:13:- Weak-* compactness needs real verification. The set of measurable simplex-valued kernels in \(\prod_{t,a}L^\infty(\mu_t)\) must be shown weak-* closed, convex, and compact; expected rewards must be represented by \(L^1(\mu_t)\) coefficients.
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:27:- Public exogenous prices. No part applies to action-dependent prices.
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:29:- Standard Borel histories and regular conditional probabilities for \(\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t})\).
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:31:- Randomized policies with private randomness independent of \(\theta\).
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:41:- “Sion/minimax applies” is currently an assertion. The policy space is infinite-dimensional and measurable; a citation or complete compact risk-set proof is needed.
./iterations/iter_005/solution_critic_01.md:49:- The corrected finite-grid identity is plausible under finite \(\Theta\), finite \(G\), randomized policies, and public exogenous prices.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:3:The idea is viable. A referee-ready no-gap theorem can be proved for fixed finite \(T\), finite grid \(G\), public exogenous prices, and common domination of the payoff-weighted reward measures. The right proof does not need an infinite-dimensional minimax theorem over policies. It should proceed through public-prefix kernels, weak-* compactness, and a finite-intersection argument.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:5:The main theorem remains conditional on the exact policy formalization. If admissible policies are Borel public-price randomized policies, one must explicitly add a completed-measure/Borel-version lemma so that weak-* limit kernels are implementable policies, not only relaxed \(L^\infty\) objects.
