./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:46:Proof route: fold all private randomness into one seed \(U\). By induction, since prices are exogenous and feedback is determined by public prices and previous own quotes, \(a_t=\alpha_t(P_{1:t},U)\). Define
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:91:1. Replace arbitrary policies by public-prefix marginal kernels.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:109:The biggest remaining proof obligation is policy-class alignment. If original policies are Borel but \(Q_t\) is an \(L^\infty\) equivalence-class object, prove that each simplex-valued \(\lambda_t\)-measurable kernel has a Borel version up to \(\lambda_t\)-null sets, and modifying on those null sets does not affect rewards.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:111:Reward-measure domination ignores histories with zero current payoff. That is valid only because prices are exogenous, rewards are additive, and future decisions are represented by separate \(q_s(P_{1:s})\).
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:122:The non-Borel label example remains the key warning for Borel policy measurability.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:132:Write the theorem in this order: policy reduction, Borel-version lemma, density representation, weak-* compactness, product compactness/no-gap, finite-prior Bayes corollary.
./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/explorer_01.md:134:Keep the theorem explicitly finite-grid and public-exogenous. Add continuum only as a separate discretization corollary.
./iterations/iter_007/consolidation.md:7:- Formalize dominated-prefix no-gap theorem via \(L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\) prefix kernels.
./iterations/iter_007/consolidation.md:20:1. Write the dominated-prefix theorem with exact policy measurability assumptions.
./iterations/iter_007/consolidation.md:21:2. Prove policy-to-prefix-kernel reduction and weak-* compactness details.
./iterations/iter_005/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:I do not see a counterexample to the corrected finite-\(\Theta\), finite-grid, randomized-policy identity under public exogenous prices. The explorer’s route is basically sound, but several claims are still proof obligations rather than established facts. The strongest correction is that compactness of the attainable reward set \(C\) is probably not the main obstacle: since \(C\subset[0,T]^\Theta\) is bounded and convex, one can apply minimax to \(\overline C\), and continuity preserves both the infimum and support functions.
./iterations/iter_005/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:7:1. **Plausible but incomplete: public-prefix reduction.**  
