./iterations/iter_007/promising_ideas.json:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The dual of l_infty may introduce finitely additive priors; measurable policy compactness may fail; the needed assumptions may be too strong to cover natural classes.","suggested_lenses":["functional-analytic minimax","compactness counterexample search","measurable policy topology"],"summary":"For infinite public-exogenous classes, investigate whether the finite-subclass modulus Phi_T equals the full grid minimax value under natural compactness or continuity assumptions. Work on fixed finite grid G_T first, define the attainable reward set in l_infty(Theta_T), and either prove a no-duality-gap theorem or construct a strict compactness-gap example.","title":"Infinite-Class No-Duality-Gap Test","why_promising":"This is the most direct route from the accepted finite-class characterization to a genuine unknown-class characterization."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The essential-sup calculation depends on conditioning on positive-probability atoms; the supremum over quotes and horizons in Delta_T(H) may expose missed contributions; bounded regret for the zero learner must be stated with the correct law-awareness and horizon convention.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample proof","tail calculation","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Formalize the solver's rare-history example: draw N with P(N=n)=c 4^{-n}, reveal a symbol s_N at time 1, then run L_N=2^n zeros followed by one spike at 1. Prove beta(h)=1 for every h, but Delta_T(H) is O(1/H) uniformly in T and the comparator value is bounded.","title":"Rare-History Beta Separation","why_promising":"It cleanly separates essential-sup raw delay tails from average value-weighted truncation, blocking a common false necessity route."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The first-hit structure across decreasing spikes is easy to mis-index; the claimed O(1/H) bound may fail for quotes near a spike threshold or for partial horizons; strict inequality P>a needs care.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic counterexample proof","value-weighted delay accounting"],"summary":"Verify or refute the strengthened tiny-price deterministic claim: blocks have L_m=2^m zeros followed by p_m=4^{-m}. Show beta(h)=1 for all finite h, while sup_T Delta_T(H)=O(1/H), not merely bounded comparator value.","title":"Tiny-Price Delta Bound","why_promising":"If correct, it gives a stronger deterministic obstruction showing even value-weighted delay loss can be small while raw beta is maximally bad."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Repeated hidden blocks can collapse because the fixed comparator averages across block types or the first reveal identifies the global type; power-tail constraints may force too little valuable mass.","suggested_lenses":["lower-bound construction","Bayes-gap computation","rate optimization"],"summary":"Build multi-scale hidden-terminal families that accumulate many qD Bayes-gap atoms without iid comparator collapse. Try nested scales D_j, probabilities q_j, and quote bands arranged so the best fixed quote remains separated across unresolved components.","title":"Multi-Scale Hidden-Terminal Lower Bounds","why_promising":"Matching the power-tail upper rate is the main missing rate result, and the one-block qD atom is known to be too weak."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Small KL or TV between terminal observations may also shrink the revenue-curve separation; after enough blocks the learner may identify the world too quickly; constants may not optimize to the desired exponent.","suggested_lenses":["Le Cam/Fano","limited-information design","posterior Bayes gap"],"summary":"Develop a limited-leakage version of repeated hidden terminals: terminal observations under different worlds have small KL/TV while their posted-price revenue curves have separated optima. Use TV/KL accumulation to lower-bound the number of unresolved rounds.","title":"Limited-Leakage Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly targets the one-reveal-learning obstruction and could turn the Bayes-gap framework into scalable lower rates."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Finite numerical examples may not scale; optimizing over priors in Psi_G can be nontrivial; observed patterns may reflect grid artifacts rather than true asymptotics.","suggested_lenses":["computational search","finite-grid exact enumeration","proof extraction"],"summary":"Use the accepted finite-grid Psi_G identity as a calculator. For small finite law classes and grids, exactly compute or optimize the least-favorable prior, compare Psi_G to delta tails, and search for families with large Bayes gap under controlled delay profiles.","title":"Psi_G Lower-Bound Calculator","why_promising":"The value identity is already proved for finite classes, so it can guide construction instead of relying on intuition about hidden delayed rewards."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The payoff a 1{a<M} is discontinuous and nonconcave; improvements may only remove logarithms; delayed feedback may force the same sqrt(T H) dependence anyway.","suggested_lenses":["upper-bound proof","posted-price learning","metric entropy"],"summary":"Investigate whether the one-dimensional posted-price structure improves the generic delayed-experts upper bound. The truncated reward functions are threshold-shaped, so chaining, continuous experts, or dynamic-pricing algorithms might reduce discretization or delay overhead.","title":"Structured Upper Bounds","why_promising":"If the existing upper rate is loose, the lower-bound target changes; this should be settled before over-investing in matching lower rates."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Closure assumptions may become artificial; predictable large delta remains harmless; constructing companion laws from a single law may be impossible without changing the model class.","suggested_lenses":["theorem formulation","necessity proof","counterexample search"],"summary":"Find natural richness conditions on unknown classes under which large value-weighted truncation loss forces a Bayes-gap lower bound. Candidate closures include mixtures, conditional variants after public histories, or hidden labels attached to delayed reward curves.","title":"When Delta Becomes Necessary","why_promising":"This could bridge the clean delta-tail sufficient theorem and the predictability-based necessary condition, yielding a scalar characterization for restricted but meaningful classes."},{"id":"idea_09","risks":"A doubling or hedging-over-horizons policy may avoid the proposed obstruction; previous-block rewards can pollute the horizon comparison; the exact anytime benchmark must be fixed first.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon-free policy analysis"],"summary":"Formalize the known-law anytime obstruction suggested in the notes: a deterministic infinite sequence with super-growing blocks and two possible deadline spikes, where any horizon-free learner has large regret on one of two horizon subsequences.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Boundary","why_promising":"The known-law oracle is horizon-aware. This direction may reveal a nontrivial fixed-process regime where tail or deadline uncertainty matters."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_008/solution_critic_02.md:3:The finite-class core is plausible: finite \(\Theta_T\), finite grid \(G\), public exogenous prices, randomized policies, grid-restricted comparator, Bayes-myopic lemma, finite-dimensional minimax, then \(T/K\) grid-to-continuum approximation.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_critic_02.md:11:- The claimed “necessary-and-sufficient characterization” is only for finite or no-gap infinite public-exogenous classes. It does not solve arbitrary unknown classes, nondominated classes, action-dependent prices, anytime policies, or high-probability/pathwise regret.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_critic_02.md:19:- Prices must be public, exogenous, and action-independent.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_critic_02.md:23:- Policies must be randomized measurable policies with private randomness independent of the active law.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_critic_02.md:35:- “Common domination should imply this” is not a theorem as written. The missing pieces are policy-to-prefix-kernel reduction, weak-* compactness, weak-* closed simplex constraints, and measurable representatives.
./iterations/iter_008/solution_critic_02.md:43:- The finite-grid minimax/Bayes identity is plausible and already supported by the ledger under finite \(\Theta\), finite \(G\), randomized policies, and grid-restricted comparator \(V_\theta^G\).
./iterations/iter_008/solution_critic_02.md:47:- The \(\delta_T(H)\) truncation upper bound is credible under exogenous public prices, with the \(H+1\)-residue Hedge proof.
./iterations/iter_010/promising_ideas.json:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"May fail if the original policy model lets delayed trade feedback carry information not already contained in public prices and private randomness; null-set conventions could force a completed-measure rather than Borel statement.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measure-theory audit"],"summary":"Prove that every admissible private-randomized Borel policy in the public exogenous model induces law-independent public-prefix marginal kernels q_t(P_1:t) on a finite quote grid, with identical expected reward coordinates L_theta.","title":"Policy-to-kernel alignment","why_promising":"This is the main remaining gap between the accepted completed L-infinity kernel no-gap theorem and the original policy model."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Borel representatives may not exist simultaneously for all dominating measures without changing the policy class; the clean theorem may need to be stated on completed measurable spaces only.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Starting from a completed L-infinity(lambda_t; Delta(G)) prefix kernel, determine exact assumptions under which it has an admissible Borel or completed-measurable implementation preserving all payoff-weighted integrals.","title":"Kernel implementation lemma","why_promising":"It isolates the reverse alignment issue and could make the infinite-class domination theorem referee-ready or identify the exact model restriction needed."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The measure-density formula must be matched to regular conditional probabilities carefully on mixture-prefix null sets; strict threshold nonattainment should stay avoided by finite G.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","formalization"],"summary":"Prove that the density support formula integral max_a sum_theta pi_theta f^theta_{t,a} d lambda_t equals the conditional Bayes envelope E max_a a P_bar_pi(M_t > a | P_1:t) for every finite prior under reward-measure domination.","title":"Density Psi identification","why_promising":"This would connect the compactness/no-gap proof to the already accepted Psi_G,F expression without relying on informal conditional-probability manipulations."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Revealing labels can accidentally change delay profiles if inserted at a payoff-relevant time or price level; the construction must keep comparator rewards and truncation losses literally matched.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Build paired classes with identical per-world beta, Delta, and delta profiles but different minimax regret: a hidden q-scaled terminal pair versus the same pair with a harmless public label revealed before the first decision.","title":"Tail-profile no-go pair","why_promising":"A clean example would prove that no scalar condition depending only on individual delay or truncation tails can characterize unknown-class learnability; observability/predictability is essential."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Naive block repetition collapses because the comparator is a single fixed quote; information may also accumulate too quickly after early reveals.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Bayes-gap computation"],"summary":"Use the finite-grid Psi value to search for multi-scale or limited-leakage lower-bound families under beta or Delta envelopes, aiming to accumulate delayed Bayes gaps across scales without iid comparator collapse.","title":"Power-tail lower rates","why_promising":"Matching the truncation upper rate is the largest open quantitative gap, and Psi_G gives a precise diagnostic for whether a proposed construction actually has large minimax value."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A meta-algorithm may pay extra delay overhead for every window, and comparator-gap terms for different H may not combine cleanly.","suggested_lenses":["upper bound","algorithm design"],"summary":"Design an adaptive multi-window algorithm that competes with the best truncation level in hindsight, targeting a bound like inf_H {sqrt(T H log K) + T/K + delta_T(H)} plus small overhead.","title":"Adaptive truncation upper bound","why_promising":"It would turn the sufficient theorem into a sharper rate formula that does not require knowing the tail profile in advance and may expose whether the current upper bound is loose."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"A doubling or horizon-mixture version of the known-law oracle may defeat the proposed obstruction; the benchmark varies with the evaluation horizon and must be handled exactly.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","model taxonomy"],"summary":"Formalize or refute the super-growing two-deadline deterministic sequence as an anytime known-law obstruction, separating horizon-aware oracle learnability from single-policy learnability over all T.","title":"Anytime known-law boundary","why_promising":"It resolves an important quantifier ambiguity left by the oracle lemma and clarifies whether fixed-law necessity becomes nontrivial again without horizon awareness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Natural examples may all be either identifiable or dominated enough for no-gap; remaining gaps may be purely pathological measurability artifacts.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","measurability"],"summary":"Search for a standard-Borel, nondominated, non-identifiable infinite class where finite subclasses have small value but the full Borel-policy class has linear value, or prove broad identifiable/analytic no-gap conditions.","title":"Natural infinite-class gaps","why_promising":"This tests whether product-closed or domination hypotheses are merely technical or genuinely necessary beyond the current non-Borel obstruction."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
