./iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:9:1. **Plausible but incomplete: policy-to-kernel reduction.**  
./iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:10:   The report writes policies as kernels \(q_{t,a}(P_{1:t})\). This needs proof. A general randomized policy may use private memory and past actions, but because prices are exogenous and rewards are additive, only the conditional marginal law of \(a_t\) given \(P_{1:t}\) matters. This reduction should be stated and proved.
./iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:13:   The compactness proof uses \(L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\) kernels. If admissible policies are required to be Borel kernels, one must argue that weak-* limit kernels have admissible Borel versions, at least up to \(\lambda_t\)-null sets. Common domination makes null-set identification safe, but this must be explicit.
./iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:36:7. **Missing assumption / worth pursuing: non-Borel label gap.**  
