./iterations/iter_007/idea_01/explorer_01.md:96:Market-model measurable gap: let \(A\subset[0,1]\) be non-Borel, \(T=2\), \(G=\{1/3,2/3\}\). World \(x\) has \(P_1=x\), and \(P_2=1\) if \(x\in A\), while \(P_2=1/2\) otherwise. Any finite subclass can be handled by a Borel policy memorizing those finitely many labels, so finite-subclass value is \(0\). But a Borel policy with regret \(<1/6\) would need to separate \(A\) by a Borel threshold set, impossible. Constant randomization gives regret \(1/6\), so the full value is \(1/6\). This is pathological but shows the compactness/measurability gap is real.
