./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:8270: Status: Valid as a compactness/measurability obstruction. The market-flavored example is pathological and depends on Borel-policy restrictions; it disappears if arbitrary nonmeasurable policies are allowed or if \(A\) is Borel.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:8272:+Iteration 8 refinement: payoff-weighted reward-measure domination fails in the non-Borel label example because the relevant class contains uncountably many singular Dirac-type prefix/reward measures. Thus it remains a stress test for any unrestricted infinite-class theorem.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:8277:+Facts: In the simple identifiable example \(T=2\), \(P_1=\theta\), \(P_2=1\), the class is easy for a Borel policy because the parameter is publicly revealed. However, finite interpolation can product-converge to arbitrary functions of \(\theta\), including non-Borel ones, so product-closedness of the Borel attainable reward set is not implied by weak/Feller continuity alone.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:8295:+Failure: Both solution critics and the gate rejected this as a full solution. The finite-class value theorem is accepted, but arbitrary infinite classes need an explicit no-gap/product-closed condition and a precise policy space. The dominated-prefix route was still conditional, not written referee-ready. The theorem is a minimax/Bayes value characterization, not a scalar tail characterization, and no matching lower bound for the power-tail upper rate is known.
./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:8301:+Failure: Iteration 8 found no proof that weak/Feller continuity alone makes the Borel-policy attainable reward set product closed. Uncountable Dirac classes can be weakly compact or Feller-continuous while failing total-variation separability and common domination. Deterministic identifiable examples may still be easy, but for a different reason: the parameter is publicly decoded, not because the reward set is compact in the needed topology.
