./logs/iter_006_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1743:iterations/iter_006/promising_ideas.json:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"May only yield a value characterization close to restating the game, and only for finite classes unless extended separately.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","model taxonomy"],"summary":"Verify the solver's finite-class iff claim carefully: for finite horizon-indexed classes Theta_T, finite grid G_T with K=T, randomized policies, public exogenous prices, and upper-regret convention, prove or refute that sublinear continuum regret is equivalent up to O(1) to (sup_pi Psi_G_T(pi))_+ = o(T). Track signed regret, positive part, and both sides of the grid-continuum comparison.","title":"Audit finite-grid value iff","why_promising":"This could turn the accepted finite-grid Bayes identity into a clean necessary-and-sufficient theorem under precise finite-class quantifiers."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The beta definition may need an infinite-process version or finite-horizon beta_T; strict threshold and block-boundary details must be checked.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample formalization","quantifier check"],"summary":"Fully formalize the deterministic tiny-price long-delay block counterexample from the solver attempt: blocks of L_m = 2^m zeros followed by spike p_m = 2^{-2m}. Show beta(h)=1 for all h, but sup_T sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a) is bounded, so the zero-quote learner has O(1) regret.","title":"Tiny-price beta counterexample","why_promising":"It would give a sharp, simple obstruction to any raw delay-probability necessity theorem, independent of stochastic estimation issues."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Need histories of positive probability for the essential supremum, and the average truncation estimates may be delicate under conditioning.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","average-vs-essential-sup"],"summary":"Construct a stochastic process where rare public histories force beta(h) to stay large, but their unconditional probability is so small that Delta_T(H)/T or delta_T(H)/T is o(1). Quantify the separation between essential-sup tail and average valuable delayed mass.","title":"Rare-history beta separation","why_promising":"This would clarify exactly why the old beta condition is stronger than the accepted truncation-loss upper theorem."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"A first informative terminal observation may reveal the global type and collapse future regret; the fixed comparator may be dominated by one scale rather than accumulating across scales.","suggested_lenses":["lower-bound construction","Bayes-gap computation"],"summary":"Build a multi-scale hidden-terminal family with a global hidden type shared across blocks, block lengths D_m, and rare terminal events q_m. Tune D_m and q_m to obey a power-tail envelope while keeping the learner uncertain across many payoff-relevant decisions; evaluate the lower bound through the finite-grid Bayes gap.","title":"Multi-scale hidden type","why_promising":"It directly attacks the main open rate gap: accumulating more than the one-block qD lower-bound atom without iid block-splicing collapse."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Separating revenue curves may necessarily make public price laws easy to distinguish; KL/TV calculations could kill the desired rate.","suggested_lenses":["Le Cam/Fano","limited-leakage design"],"summary":"Replace exact terminal revelation by noisy or partial terminal observations. Design two or many environments whose quote revenue curves have separated optima but whose public histories have small TV/KL over many blocks. Use Le Cam, Fano, or Assouad plus the Bayes-gap identity to prove regret lower bounds.","title":"Limited-leakage lower bounds","why_promising":"This is a plausible route to matching power-tail rates while avoiding one-reveal learning."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Single-threshold price observations generate highly structured reward vectors, so arbitrary delayed-experts lower bounds may not embed.","suggested_lenses":["reduction attempt","upper-bound critique"],"summary":"Test whether the sqrt(T H log K) term in the truncation upper bound is necessary for this market structure. Try to embed a bounded-delay full-information experts lower bound into threshold-hitting rewards with beta(H)=0, or prove that the threshold structure permits a faster algorithm.","title":"Tightness of delayed-learning term","why_promising":"If embeddable, it validates a major part of the current rate. If not, the known upper rate may be improvable."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Infinite-dimensional policy compactness and measurability can become technical; finite-subclass lower bounds may not imply an upper algorithm for the full class.","suggested_lenses":["compactness","finite-subclass approximation"],"summary":"Extend the finite Theta, finite G minimax/Bayes identity to infinite exogenous classes, or identify the right replacement. Candidate route: finite-subclass lower bounds plus total boundedness under a reward-curve metric, or a compact closure of attainable reward vectors in l_infinity(Theta).","title":"Infinite-class extension","why_promising":"The original problem is class-level, not necessarily finite. A clean extension would make the value characterization more than a finite toy theorem."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"A horizon-free learner may use doubling or hedging across deadlines; the proposed two-deadline inequality must be embedded into one infinite process without later blocks offsetting regret.","suggested_lenses":["anytime lower bound","known-law boundary"],"summary":"Formalize or refute the candidate anytime obstruction: a known deterministic sequence with super-growing blocks containing an early 1/2 spike and later 1 spike. Show whether every horizon-free policy has linear regret along one of two horizon subsequences, despite the known-law horizon-aware oracle lemma.","title":"Anytime known-law boundary","why_promising":"It would clarify whether horizon awareness is an essential assumption or just an artifact of the oracle proof."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
